# diagnostics.tkr — the CHECKER-STAGE compile-fail CHANNEL.
#
# The project it names is still here and still asserted (`m1::PRIV`, #594 crumb 8c / issue #613),
# and it now carries the `member_const_visibility_leak_rejected` regression too (`leakbase`/
# `leaksub`, #594 crumb 7) — that project became the PARSE-stage host and can no longer reach the
# checker at all, so its assertion had to move to a build that does. Around those two, THIRTY-EIGHT
# further rejected constructs live one per namespace under `src/` (thirty-seven at the all-
# diagnostics ruling, plus `c61_unsafe_alias_rejected` folded in by cargo/20-unsafe-alias-rejeitado),
# each a fixture that used to be a `Given source inline` snippet in regressor.tkr paying for its OWN
# failing build.
#
# WHY THEY SHARE ONE BUILD (owner ruling 2026-07-25 — "somente um build é necessário"): the runner
# already supports it. A compile-fail scenario that declares NO `Given source`/`Given source inline`
# is checked against the PROJECT's own failing build, and that build is cached across every scenario
# of this file (`tkr_compile_fail_artifact` → `tkr_ensure_built`). The compiler reports EVERY
# diagnostic it finds rather than stopping at the first, so one build emits all forty and each
# scenario below pins its own. Measured, not assumed: 40 scenarios, 1 build.
#
# WHY TWO MORE FOLDED IN (issue #68). `c55_unsafe_field_in_safe_struct` and `c53_reject_flags_65`
# were two of the four `cases/*.tks` rows that used to buy their own build, because their
# diagnostics come from a checker PRE-WALK pass that stopped at the first error. Those two passes
# (`validate_type_decls` and `check_modules`) are SOFT boundaries — they produce nothing the item
# walk reads — so they now collect and the build carries on. They are namespaces of the shared build.
#
# WHY ONE `cases/*.tks` ROW SURVIVES (`prewalk_signature_stops.tks`). The remaining two constructs
# are rejected by the checker's SIGNATURE walk, a HARD boundary: a declaration whose signature is
# rejected contributes no binding, so continuing would report every caller as "undefined" and blame
# the caller for the callee's defect (M.3). The signature walk therefore collects everything IT sees
# and cuts. Both constructs sit in ONE file and are reported by ONE build, and a standalone `Given
# source` build is cached per (file, declared env) — `regr_src_key`. Residual: one build, was four.
# See docs/design/all-diagnostics-pre-walk-0.3.1.md §3 for every pass's boundary.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT THAT DECIDED IT, kept because it is the reason the remaining row is NOT folded and
# because it refutes the obvious-looking move. Taken while all four still stopped at the first error:
#
#   * put all four in a project of their own and the build reports exactly ONE diagnostic; remove
#     that one and the build reports the next, alone — a pass that STOPS makes four cases in one
#     build one diagnostic, not four;
#   * fold ONE of them into THIS project and the build's diagnostic count falls from FIFTY to ONE.
#     Every scenario below would have gone red to save a single build.
#
# Two of the four stopped being a problem by being FIXED, not by being folded: their passes now
# collect. The surviving row is the one whose boundary is HARD by design, so folding it would still
# cost the fifty. It stays a build until the signature walk can collect too — the all-diagnostics
# work, not this channel's — and it is the whole residual between this channel's cost and one.
#
# WHY THE `F8 target` ROWS ARE NOT FOLDED EITHER, and the reason is stronger than "a differing
# configuration is a different build". Their subject is the diagnostic `unsupported TEKO_TARGET`,
# which the build emits only AFTER the front end has succeeded — measured: building this project with
# `TEKO_TARGET=x86_64-solaris` reports the thirty-eight CHECKER rejections and never reaches the
# target error at all. A build whose checker must fail and a build whose checker must pass are
# mutually exclusive; those three rows share ONE build with each other (`regr_src_key` caches on
# (file, declared env)) and can share none with this one.
Feature: the two cross-namespace visibility rejections, in one build

  Scenario: the root namespace may not read another namespace's private const, even namespace-qualified
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "is private to"

  Scenario: a subclass's own namespace may not read a private const inherited from its base's namespace
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "member const `SECRET` is private to `Base`"

